        <body>
            <div xml:id="d181">

                <pb facs="0489" n="399" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg399"/>

                <head>No. 177.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Cadwalader to Mr. Avery.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-08-11"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 11,
                        1875</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 61.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 64, of the 12th June,
                    inclosing a copy of correspondence with the Tsungli Yamen, relative to the
                    emigration-laws of the United States, has been received.</p>
                <p>The Department approves the friendly spirit manifested by you in furnishing the
                    information sought, and is convinced of the desirability of impressing on the
                    Chinese government on all proper occasions the care taken by this Government to
                    prevent wrong-doing and injury to Chinese as in the cooly-traffic, and the
                    desire to freely receive and liberally treat honest Chinese emigrants.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN L. CADWALADER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                            Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
